{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\nII. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\nIII. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133 :question/math-academy-id "q-287966" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x=x+1$\n- **II.** $2x+1=2x+1$\n- **III.** $2x+1=2x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =x+1-x \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x=x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+1-2x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x+1=2x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+3 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+3-2x \\\\ 1 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111138 :question/math-academy-id "q-288419" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3x=2x+5$\n- **II.** $4x-2=4x-2$\n- **III.** $5x+3=5x+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2x+5 \\\\ 3x-2x & =2x+5-2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x=2x+5$ has the unique solution $x=5.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =4x-2 \\\\ 4x-2-4x & =4x-2-4x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x-2=4x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =5x+1 \\\\ 5x+3-5x & =5x+1-5x \\\\ 3 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181 :question/math-academy-id "q-288425" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- **II.** $6x+1=6x+5$\n- **III.** $7x-4=6x-6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182 :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x-5=x-2$\n- **II.** $6x+1=6x+9$\n- **III.** $-4x+3=-4x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196 :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3x+2=4x+1$\n- **II.** $2x-5=3x-3$\n- **III.** $5x-4=5x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492000 :question/math-academy-id "q-288426" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4x+2=4x+2$\n\n- **II.** $-2x-1=-2x-1$\n\n- **III.** $3x+5=3x+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & =4x+2 \\\\ 4x+2-4x & =4x+2-4x \\\\ 2 & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x+2=4x+2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-1 & =-2x-1 \\\\ -2x-1+2x & =-2x-1+2x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x-1=-2x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & =3x+9 \\\\ 3x+5-3x & =3x+9-3x \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492056 :question/math-academy-id "q-288431" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3x+4=3x+4$\n\n- **II.** $x-5=x-2$\n\n- **III.** $2x+3=x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4 & =3x+4 \\\\ 3x+4-3x & =3x+4-3x \\\\ 4 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x+4=3x+4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ -5 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =x+3 \\\\ 2x+3-x & =x+3-x \\\\ x+3 & =3 \\\\ x+3-3 & =3-3 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x+3=x+3$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111134 :question/math-academy-id "q-287970" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2px=3x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135 :question/math-academy-id "q-287971" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(p-3)x=4x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297170 :question/math-academy-id "q-287975" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p-3,\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p-3=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297183 :question/math-academy-id "q-288436" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5px=10x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184 :question/math-academy-id "q-288440" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n-4kx=8x\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-4k,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-4k=8.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476179 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(6p-6)x=36x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=6p-6,\\,b=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n6p-6=36.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6p & =42 \\\\ p & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476183 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5p-3)x=37x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p-3,\\,b=37.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p-3=37.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p & =40 \\\\ p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476185 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(3p-1)x=17x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=3p-1,\\,b=17.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n3p-1=17.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =18 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476189 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(7p-1)x=20x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p-1,\\,b=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p-1=20.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p & =21 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476193 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7px=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491998 :question/math-academy-id "q-288570" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-t-2)x=x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-t-2,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-t-2=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & =3 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549009 :question/math-academy-id "q-288561" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(t+4)x=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=t+4,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nt+4=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549054 :question/math-academy-id "q-288560" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(r-7)x=-x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=r-7,\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nr-7=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553489 :question/math-academy-id "q-288441" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7mx=-14x\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7m,\\,b=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7m=-14.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555761 :question/math-academy-id "q-288569" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(4r-6)x=22x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=4r-6,\\,b=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n4r-6=22.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4r & =28 \\\\ r & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111136 :question/math-academy-id "q-287978" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(p - 3)x + q = 4x + 1$\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p + q$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111137 :question/math-academy-id "q-287985" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(p - 5)x + 2q = 2x + 6$\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p + q$?$p + q =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194 :question/math-academy-id "q-288583" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195 :question/math-academy-id "q-288584" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198 :question/math-academy-id "q-288622" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297199 :question/math-academy-id "q-288623" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2k=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{9}{2}$ and $r=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=\\frac{9}{2}+6=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200 :question/math-academy-id "q-288627" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477006 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+7=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=88.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=2$ and $n=11,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=2+11=13.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477007 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+16=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=9.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=9$ and $t=6,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=9+6=15.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477016 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+15=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=12.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n4t=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=12$ and $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=12+1=13.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477018 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+8=19.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=48.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=11$ and $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=11+6=17.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477021 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5tx+(1-m)=55x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n5tx+(1-m)=55x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n5t=55.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=11.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=11$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=11+(-3)=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492013 :question/math-academy-id "q-288574" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+2)x+r=5x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+2)x+r=5x+3\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+2=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nr=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=3$ and $r=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=3+3=6.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :topic/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}
